<h2>A Spanish patentee, a Gujarat tender, a Delhi suit</h2>
<p>Sotkon, a Spanish company holding Indian Patent IN&rsquo;620 for a sub-surface refuse collection system, sued four defendants — a Mumbai transporter, a Delhi infrastructure company, an Ahmedabad joint venture and the Gandhinagar Municipal Corporation — alleging that their bid for Gandhinagar&rsquo;s smart underground bins tender infringed its patent and copied its brochure drawings. Venue was pleaded on two footings: Section 20(b) CPC, through the Delhi defendant, and Section 62 of the Copyright Act, through the plaintiff&rsquo;s claimed principal place of business in Delhi.</p>

<h2>The two pleas, tested separately</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Section 62 shortcut, closed.</strong> The special copyright venue cannot carry patent reliefs: <em>&ldquo;Jurisdiction needs to be established for each relief prayed for.&rdquo;</em> A composite IP suit does not acquire the most convenient venue among its causes of action.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Section 20(b) route, unperfected.</strong> Suing outstation defendants through one local defendant needs leave of the court or acquiescence. No leave was sought for the Mumbai, Ahmedabad and Gandhinagar defendants, and their written statement disputed jurisdiction from the outset.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The cause of action, elsewhere.</strong> The tender, the bid and the municipal works all belonged to Gandhinagar: <em>&ldquo;Nothing has actually happened within the jurisdiction of this Court.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Forum conveniens, applied.</strong> Even if a sliver of the cause of action had arisen in Delhi, the Court held this a fit case to decline leave — the dispute&rsquo;s centre of gravity was Gujarat.</div>
</div>

<h2>The burden, and where it was fixed</h2>
<p>The judgment restates a point that composite commercial suits often blur: <em>&ldquo;the burden of proof for establishing territorial jurisdiction lies on the plaintiff.&rdquo;</em> The Court worked through the familiar line — Sanjay Dalia, Patel Roadways, Dhodha House and Ultra Home on the interplay of general and special venue provisions, and its own recent statement of principles — before holding that the plaint must be returned for presentation before a court of competent jurisdiction. For Sotkon, the result is procedural, not terminal: the claim survives, but it must be fought where the tender lives.</p>

<h2>Filing strategy in commercial IP matters</h2>
<p>Three lessons travel beyond this batch. First, plead the jurisdictional facts relief-by-relief: a table in the plaint mapping each cause of action to its venue foundation forces the analysis the court will anyway perform. Second, where outstation defendants are necessary parties, apply for leave under Section 20(b) at institution — its absence is an unforced error that no later argument cures once the written statement contests venue. Third, remember that the Commercial Courts Act changed case management, not venue: the tests remain those of the CPC and the special statutes, and a returned plaint in year five is the price of optimism in year one.</p>
<div class="note">The judgment decides forum, not infringement; no view on the merits of the patent or copyright claims follows from the return of the plaint. Case details are as recorded in the batch judgment dated 19 November 2026, of which CS(COMM) 484/2021 is the lead-listed matter.</div>
